WEBVTT

00:03.920 --> 00:04.400
All right.

00:04.400 --> 00:06.360
So what is a rank function in SQL?

00:06.360 --> 00:09.720
The rank function is going to go and assign for each row a number a rank.

00:10.120 --> 00:12.640
And this time it's going to go and handle the ties.

00:12.880 --> 00:18.400
So that means if in your data you have two rows having the same values, they are going to share the

00:18.400 --> 00:19.520
same ranking.

00:19.760 --> 00:24.360
One thing about the ranking function is that it's going to go and leave gaps in the ranking.

00:24.520 --> 00:30.160
So there is possibility of skipping ranks in order to understand how the rank function works in SQL,

00:30.400 --> 00:32.000
we're going to have a very simple example.

00:32.040 --> 00:35.120
All right so again with the same data but with different function.

00:35.120 --> 00:36.520
So our window looks like this.

00:36.560 --> 00:40.400
It starts with the function rank doesn't accept any argument inside it.

00:40.400 --> 00:41.720
Then we have the window like this.

00:41.720 --> 00:45.320
Order by sales descending from the highest to the lowest.

00:45.320 --> 00:47.560
And our data is already sorted like that.

00:47.560 --> 00:49.840
So now how is SQL going to go and assign the ranks?

00:49.840 --> 00:51.840
The first row is going to be the highest rank.

00:51.840 --> 00:53.560
So the value 100 is going to be one.

00:53.560 --> 00:55.360
Then the second one is going to be two.

00:55.400 --> 00:56.680
But now for the third one.

00:56.680 --> 01:00.000
As you can see we have here two values that are the same.

01:00.000 --> 01:01.160
So we have a tie.

01:01.160 --> 01:03.210
day and this time is going to go.

01:03.210 --> 01:06.370
And as well let them to share the same rank.

01:06.370 --> 01:09.050
So both of them are going to be the rank two.

01:09.090 --> 01:11.370
So it's not like the row number where we have over here.

01:11.410 --> 01:14.730
Three this time we have two because we have a tie.

01:14.770 --> 01:18.770
So having same values means they are going to share the same rank.

01:18.810 --> 01:23.210
And now moving to the next value going to be tricky one, because if you check over here you can see

01:23.210 --> 01:25.610
that the next rank should be like the three.

01:25.650 --> 01:25.890
Right.

01:25.890 --> 01:27.130
So we have one two.

01:27.170 --> 01:30.610
And then the next value that generated in the rank should be three.

01:30.650 --> 01:34.930
But SQL going to say you know what this value position going to be number four.

01:34.930 --> 01:37.290
So as you can see 1234.

01:37.570 --> 01:39.930
So actually the position number here is four.

01:40.090 --> 01:42.930
And SQL is going to go and give it the rank of four.

01:43.170 --> 01:46.570
So with that SQL is going to be leaving a gap in the ranking.

01:46.570 --> 01:49.450
You can see we are skipping the rank number three.

01:49.490 --> 01:53.730
And this always happens once you have a tie where you are sharing the same ranking.

01:53.970 --> 01:55.530
So for the next one it's going to be easy.

01:55.530 --> 01:57.410
It's going to be the row number five.

01:57.450 --> 02:02.500
So now by looking to the output of the rank function you can see that we don't have a unique ranking

02:02.500 --> 02:02.740
here.

02:02.740 --> 02:07.140
We have shared ranking in case of the tie so it handles the ties.

02:07.340 --> 02:11.780
But here we have gaps in the ranks, so we are skipping ranks.

02:11.780 --> 02:15.260
When I think about the rank function I think about the Olympics.

02:15.460 --> 02:21.500
If two athletes tie for the gold medal the first place, there will be no silver medal for the second

02:21.500 --> 02:22.140
place.

02:22.140 --> 02:25.860
The next medal going to be given to the bronze to the third place.

